# With firmware RPS and the Linux RPS fan-out, bursts to host-terminated
# flows land on the per-CPU receive backlogs; the 1000-packet default
# overflows at line-rate bursts (softnet_stat column-2 drops). 4096 absorbs
# them with no measured drops at ~1.5 Gbit/s offered.
net.core.netdev_max_backlog=4096

# Default RPS mask applied to every rx queue at creation, so it needs no
# re-application when a netdev is recreated. Mask 'e' = cores 1-3: every ipq807x
# is a quad A53, and core 0 is left to the N2H queue-0 NAPI that firmware RPS
# hashes host-terminated flows onto.
# This service runs at START=11, before netifd, so it covers every delivery netdev
# created afterwards: the bridges, pppoe-wan and the IGS ifb devices. The wired
# ports exist before it runs and keep the kernel default, which is correct - under
# offload their traffic never reaches a host softirq.
#
# The Wi-Fi VAPs are deliberately NOT ours to set. OpenWrt's own packet_steering
# service owns rps_cpus for every netdev that has a device symlink and no lower_*
# - on ipq807x the Wi-Fi VAPs and the EDMA conduit netdev - and it re-runs on
# every interface.* event. We set network.globals.packet_steering='0'
# (98-nss-offload, and qca-nss-ecm's init forces it again on every start and on
# its network/packet_steering reload triggers), and in that mode it writes 0 to
# those queues - so any VAP rps_cpus we set is erased again within a second.
#
# That override is measured, not assumed. Leaving the service at its default
# puts the VAPs on core 3, the EDMA conduit on core 2 and the conduit NAPI
# thread on core 0. On a Wi-Fi client to wired host flow through the router,
# two runs each: with the service disabled 749 and 800 Mbit/s and no n2h queue
# drops; with it at its default 619 and 626 Mbit/s and 158 then 83 fresh
# n2h_rx_queue drops. Its placement fights firmware RPS, which already hashes
# host-terminated flows onto the N2H queues this mask is shaped around.
#
# Do not add a writer to fight the service either - configure the service, as
# 98-nss-offload does.
net.core.rps_default_mask=e
